     1	# The Catch: writing guidelines
     2	
     3	House style for every reader-facing page. This is the voice of the institution,
     4	not of any person. It binds generated and hand-written copy equally; the
     5	enforcement section says which rules the build gate holds and which an editor
     6	holds.
     7	
     8	## Who is on the page
     9	
    10	A careful person showing you documents. Not a pundit, not an analyst desk, not
    11	a brand. The register is plain declarative English that trusts the reader's
    12	intelligence and assumes none of their vocabulary. Confidence comes from the
    13	records, so the prose never performs confidence; where the record is thin the
    14	page says so in plain words.
    15	
    16	## The reader
    17	
    18	Smart, curious, has never read a BLS release or a Fed statement. Brings
    19	everyday words (job, paycheck, price, loan, rent) and nothing else. Every
    20	other term is ours to introduce.
    21	
    22	## What each view is for (2026-09-08, Zain)
    23	
    24	The story is the synthesis: tight reporting in the register of the Zika page, most
    25	important first, each paragraph one level deeper, mechanisms explained in the sentence
    26	that needs them, quotes as cards each introduced by the paragraph before it and headed
    27	with the speaker's name, dated steps as a chronology, numbers as figures, and a
    28	"so what" closing every section. It is compact. A raw record (the filing line by line, a
    29	committee's books, a ballot listing) is not story; it is marked `detail` on its
    30	`SourcedBlock` and appears in Just the facts and Show the work, where the reader who wants
    31	the document finds it in the open, never collapsed. Show the work adds the proof.
    32	
    33	A page is never finished. Records arrive after publication (a transcript, a roll call, a
    34	later filing); each one is folded in the same day it is captured, the claim it bears on is
    35	re-checked, and the What happened next section carries the dated addition. "We do not
    36	hold it" is a to-do with an owner and a date, not a state.
    37	
    38	## One voice per layer
    39	
    40	The three-projection pages type every block fact / narrative / proof. Each
    41	layer keeps its own register and never borrows the others':
    42	
    43	- **Fact layer.** Declarative sentences, dated, sourced. Every number carries
    44	  its comparison (what was expected, what came before, what is normal), per
    45	  the holistic-facts rule: a bare number is not yet a fact a reader can use.
    46	  Signification is allowed when the records carry it ("fell for the wrong
    47	  reason: fewer people working or looking" is a fact; the participation
    48	  numbers are the reason). No adjective of size or speed unless computed
    49	  ("largest since March 2020", never "massive").
    50	- **Narrative layer.** The explainer voice. It teaches mechanisms (two
    51	  surveys, seasonal adjustment), builds the arc, and says what the numbers
    52	  mean for the people in them. It may carry judgment only when the fact layer
    53	  underneath supports it, and it names what it will not do ("we will not
    54	  guess at one").
    55	- **Proof layer.** Auditor register, unapologetic. Verbatim quotes, series
    56	  codes, capture dates, hashes. Never simplified: the reader who opened
    57	  "Show the work" asked for the machinery.
    58	
    59	## Grounding: introduce before use
    60	
    61	Every concept is either a prerequisite (the everyday-words list above) or is
    62	introduced on the page before any sentence leans on it. Introducing means
    63	landing the idea and the term together, once, at first use, in apposition or
    64	one short sentence ("U-6, the broadest underutilization measure the agency
    65	publishes"). After introduction the term is used freely. One introduction per
    66	page; a page that introduces more than five or six new terms is trying to be
    67	two pages.
    68	
    69	## Plain language, precision mode
    70	
    71	Plainness never changes the claim. A term of art that IS the concept stays
    72	and gets grounded; decorative jargon goes. Short common words over fancy
    73	ones, active sentences, one claim per sentence, run-ons broken up. "Payrolls
    74	fell by 23,000" beats every longer way of saying it.
    75	
    76	## Contrast must be measured
    77	
    78	Contrast is one of our main tools (expected vs. actual, first estimate vs.
    79	current, headline vs. broad measure) and it is earned only when both sides
    80	are measured things from the records. "The rate fell because the labor force
    81	shrank, not because more people found work" contrasts two measured series.
    82	The rhetorical flip ("this isn't X, it's Y") that sets up a strawman to
    83	knock down is banned; state the real thing directly.
    84	
    85	## Causes are quoted or absent
    86	
    87	We never author a cause. A cause appears on the page only as a quote from a
    88	record (with its author named) or not at all, and the absence is said plainly
    89	("the release states the figure without a cause, and we will not guess at
    90	one"). When an outlet authors a cause the record does not contain, that gap
    91	is itself reportable, attributed to the outlet.
    92	
    93	## No template skeletons
    94	
    95	The corpus-level tell is a recurring scaffold: every section shaped the same,
    96	stock sentences recurring across pages ("Key indicators to monitor…", "The
    97	analysis notes…", "It remains to be seen…"). Structure follows the story.
    98	Section headings are written for the page, not reused as slots. If two
    99	articles could swap a paragraph without anyone noticing, that paragraph is
   100	template, and template is the one voice we never use. The "what we do not
   101	know yet" section names concrete observables ("a second month of a shrinking
   102	labor force"), never analyst boilerplate.
   103	
   104	## The skim layer carries the story
   105	
   106	Headline, dek, KPI strip, and Three Things must work alone: a reader who
   107	stops there leaves with the story, correctly. Three Things are whole ideas
   108	with their comparisons folded in, not teasers.
   109	
   110	## Mechanical bans (gate-enforceable)
   111	
   112	- No em dashes in body prose. Colon, comma, parentheses, or two sentences.
   113	- No rhetorical negation-flip (see Contrast above).
   114	- Banned vocabulary in reader-facing text: significant, notably, crucially,
   115	  importantly, robust, comprehensive, landscape, dynamics, delve, underscore,
   116	  highlight (as a verb of emphasis), "it's worth noting", "it is important
   117	  to", "serves as", "plays a role", "in today's". If the word carries a
   118	  measurable meaning, replace it with the measurement.
   119	- No hedge stacks: one qualifier per claim, chosen to match the evidence.
   120	  Unverified is said as "we have not verified this", not "may potentially
   121	  suggest".
   122	- No adjective triplets; no "Similarly / Likewise / In the same way"
   123	  transitions (a transition states what the next thing adds).
   124	- No unnamed attribution: "analysts say", "experts argue", "observers note"
   125	  never appear. Every voiced view carries a name and a record, or it is cut.
   126	  This is the site's whole premise applied to its own prose.
   127	- No trailing significance clauses: the "-ing" tail that inflates a fact
   128	  ("…, highlighting the growing importance of…", "…, reflecting broader
   129	  trends…"). State the fact; the reader judges its size.
   130	- Repeat the noun. Rotating synonyms for one thing (the release / the report /
   131	  the document / the publication) to avoid repetition trades clarity for
   132	  variety. One thing, one name, used every time.
   133	- Internal vocabulary never reaches the reader (already gated): tier names,
   134	  claim ids, enum values, pipeline terms.
   135	
   136	## What this is not
   137	
   138	- Not Zain's personal voice. Personal signatures (slash groupings, controlled
   139	  roughness, first-person war stories) belong to a byline, and The Catch's
   140	  pages carry none.
   141	- Not smart brevity. We compress with the skim layer, not with axioms and
   142	  bolded labels.
   143	- Not the analyst-brief register (hedged passive summarizing, "reports
   144	  indicate", per-section methodology disclaimers). Our confidence statements
   145	  live in the proof layer and the methodology page, stated once, plainly.
   146	
   147	## Enforcement
   148	
   149	Literal source titles and quotations retain their original wording. House-style
   150	scans apply to text we write, including generated event and slot labels; topic
   151	words such as oil pipelines or heat waves are not internal system vocabulary.
   152	
   153	Build gate (check-events.mjs) holds: internal vocabulary, jammed inline tags,
   154	layer typing, citation resolution, and can hold the em-dash and
   155	banned-vocabulary scans next. Editor (or reviewing agent) holds: grounding,
   156	one-voice-per-layer, measured contrast, template detection, the skim-layer
   157	test. A page fails review if a cold reader of only the first three sentences
   158	cannot say what happened and why it matters to them.

