### data/sources/fed-rate/fomc-statement-2026-09-16.txt
     1  Federal Reserve Board - Federal Reserve issues FOMC statement
     2  
     3  September 16, 2026
     4  Federal Reserve issues FOMC statement
     5  For release at 2:00 p.m. EDT
     6  The Federal Open Market Committee approved the following statement for release by a 12 – 0 vote:
     7  The Committee decided to raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point to 3-3/4 to 4 percent, in support of the Federal Reserve's dual mandate. The Committee is continuing its policy of maintaining ample reserves in the banking system.
     8  Economic activity is expanding at a solid pace. While uncertainty remains elevated owing, in part, to geopolitical developments, domestic spending has been resilient. Productivity growth is strong, and capital investment is robust. Job gains have kept pace with the workforce, and the unemployment rate has changed little.
     9  Inflation remains elevated. Today's policy action will support a timelier return to the Committee's 2 percent goal. The Committee will deliver price stability.
    10  For media inquiries, please email [email protected] or call 202-452-2955.

### data/sources/fed-rate/fomc-implementation-2026-09-16.txt
     1  Federal Reserve Board - Implementation Note issued September 16, 2026
     2  
     3  Press Release
     4  September 16, 2026
     5  Implementation Note issued September 16, 2026
     6  Decisions Regarding Monetary Policy Implementation
     7  The Federal Reserve has made the following decisions to implement the monetary policy stance announced by the Federal Open Market Committee in its statement on September 16, 2026:
     8  -
     9  The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System voted unanimously to raise the interest rate paid on reserve balances to 3.90 percent, effective September 17, 2026.
    10  - As part of its policy decision, the Federal Open Market Committee voted to direct the Open Market Desk at the Federal Reserve Bank of New York, until instructed otherwise, to execute transactions in the System Open Market Account in accordance with the following domestic policy directive:
    11  "Effective September 17, 2026, the Federal Open Market Committee directs the Desk to: - Undertake open market operations as necessary to maintain the federal funds rate in a target range of 3-3/4 to 4 percent.
    12  - Conduct standing overnight repurchase agreement operations at a rate of 4.0 percent.
    13  - Conduct standing overnight reverse repurchase agreement operations at an offering rate of 3.75 percent and with a per-counterparty limit of $160 billion per day.
    14  - When appropriate, increase the System Open Market Account holdings of securities through purchases of Treasury bills and, if needed, other Treasury securities with remaining maturities of 3 years or less to maintain an ample level of reserves.
    15  - Roll over at auction all principal payments from the Federal Reserve's holdings of Treasury securities. Reinvest all principal payments from the Federal Reserve's holdings of agency securities into Treasury bills."
    16  -
    17  In a related action, the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System voted unanimously to approve a 1/4 percentage point increase in the primary credit rate to 4.0 percent, effective September 17, 2026. In taking this action, the Board approved requests to establish that rate submitted by the Board of Directors of the Federal Reserve Banks of Cleveland, Richmond, Atlanta, Chicago, Minneapolis, Kansas City, and Dallas.
    18  This information will be updated as appropriate to reflect decisions of the Federal Open Market Committee or the Board of Governors regarding details of the Federal Reserve's operational tools and approach used to implement monetary policy.
    19  More information regarding open market operations and reinvestments may be found on the Federal Reserve Bank of New York's website.

### data/sources/fed-rate/fomc-statement-2026-07-29.txt
     1  Federal Reserve Board - Federal Reserve issues FOMC statement
     2  
     3  July 29, 2026
     4  Federal Reserve issues FOMC statement
     5  For release at 2:00 p.m. EDT
     6  The Federal Open Market Committee approved the following statement for release by a 9 – 3 vote:
     7  The Committee decided to maintain the target range for the federal funds rate at 3-1/2 to 3-3/4 percent, in support of the Federal Reserve's dual mandate. The Committee is continuing its policy of maintaining ample reserves in the banking system.
     8  Economic activity is expanding at a solid pace despite elevated uncertainty that owes, in part, to the conflict in the Middle East. Productivity growth and capital investment are strong. Job gains have kept pace with the workforce, and the unemployment rate has changed little.
     9  Inflation remains elevated relative to the Committee's 2 percent goal, in part reflecting supply shocks that have driven price increases in certain sectors, including energy. The Committee will deliver price stability.
    10  Voting against the monetary policy action were Beth M. Hammack, Neel Kashkari, and Lorie K. Logan, who preferred to raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point at this meeting.
    11  For media inquiries, please email [email protected] or call 202-452-2955.

### data/sources/fed-rate/bls-cpi-2026-09-11.txt
     1  Consumer Price Index News Release - 2026 M08 Results
     2  
     3  Transmission of material in this release is embargoed until
     4  8:30 a.m. (ET) Friday, September 11, 2026 USDL-26-1496
     5  Technical information: (202) 691-7000 * cpi_info@bls.gov * www.bls.gov/cpi
     6  Media contact: (202) 691-5902 * PressOffice@bls.gov
     7  CONSUMER PRICE INDEX - AUGUST 2026
     8  The Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers (CPI-U) increased 0.4 percent on a seasonally adjusted basis in August
     9  after rising 0.1 percent in July, the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics reported today. Over the last 12 months, the all
    10  items index increased 3.4 percent before seasonal adjustment.
    11  The index for gasoline rose 3.9 percent in August, accounting for over one third of the monthly all items increase. The
    12  index for energy increased 2.1 percent over the month. The shelter index rose 0.3 percent in August after rising 0.1
    13  percent in July. The index for food increased 0.1 percent over the month, as the index for food away from home increased
    14  0.3 percent.
    15  The index for all items less food and energy rose 0.3 percent after increasing 0.2 percent in July. Indexes that
    16  increased over the month include communication, lodging away from home, airline fares, education, and used cars and
    17  trucks. Conversely, the index for medical care and the index for motor vehicle insurance were among the major indexes
    18  that decreased in August.
    19  The all items index rose 3.4 percent for the 12 months ending August as it did for the 12 months ending July. The all
    20  items less food and energy index rose 2.4 percent over the year, following a 2.5-percent increase over the 12 months
    21  ending July. The energy index increased 16.3 percent for the 12 months ending August. The food index increased 2.7
    22  percent over the last year.
    23  Food
    24  The food index rose 0.1 percent in August, as it did in July. The index for food at home was unchanged over the month.
    25  Four of the six major grocery store food group indexes increased in August. The meats, poultry, fish, and eggs index
    26  increased 0.1 percent over the month, as the eggs index rose 2.9 percent. The index for other food at home also
    27  increased 0.1 percent in August. The nonalcoholic beverages index increased 0.2 percent over the month, and the dairy
    28  and related products index rose 0.3 percent. The index for cereals and bakery products was unchanged in August.
    29  In contrast, the fruits and vegetables index declined 0.4 percent in August after falling in the preceding 2 months. The
    30  index for lettuce continued to fall, declining 6.2 percent in August after decreasing 16.4 percent in July.
    31  The food away from home index rose 0.3 percent in August. The index for full service meals rose 0.4 percent, and the
    32  index for limited service meals was unchanged over the month.
    33  The food at home index rose 2.2 percent over the 12 months ending in August. The index for other food at home increased
    34  2.4 percent, and the index for fruits and vegetables rose 3.2 percent over the year. The nonalcoholic beverages index
    35  increased 3.7 percent over the 12 months ending in August, and the cereals and bakery products index rose 2.6 percent
    36  over the same period. The index for meats, poultry, fish, and eggs increased 1.1 percent over the last 12 months. In
    37  contrast, the dairy and related products index fell 0.3 percent over the year.
    38  The food away from home index rose 3.4 percent over the last year. The index for full service meals rose 3.5 percent,
    39  and the index for limited service meals rose 3.2 percent over the 12 months ending in August.
    40  Energy
    41  The index for energy increased 2.1 percent in August after falling 1.5 percent in July. The gasoline index increased 3.9
    42  percent over the month. (Before seasonal adjustment, gasoline prices increased 2.5 percent in August.) Conversely, the
    43  index for natural gas decreased 1.1 percent in August, and the index for electricity fell 0.2 percent.
    44  The index for energy increased 16.3 percent over the past 12 months due in large part to the index for gasoline rising
    45  27.4 percent over the same period. The natural gas index increased 4.4 percent over the 12 months ending in August, and
    46  the electricity index rose 3.8 percent.
    47  All items less food and energy
    48  The index for all items less food and energy rose 0.3 percent in August after rising 0.2 percent in July. The shelter
    49  index increased 0.3 percent over the month after rising 0.1 percent in July. The index for owners' equivalent rent rose
    50  0.2 percent in August as did the index for rent. The lodging away from home index rose 2.4 percent in August after
    51  falling 2.8 percent the previous month.
    52  The index for communication increased 2.3 percent over the month after rising 0.6 percent in July. The airline fares
    53  index rose 2.7 percent in August, and the education index increased 0.8 percent. The index for used cars and trucks
    54  increased 0.4 percent in August, and the index for new vehicles rose 0.3 percent. The personal care index and the
    55  household furnishings and operations index also increased over the month.
    56  The medical care index decreased 0.2 percent in August after rising 0.4 percent in July. The index for dental services
    57  fell 0.6 percent over the month. The hospital services index, physicians' services index, and prescription drugs index
    58  were all unchanged in August.
    59  The motor vehicle insurance index declined 0.8 percent in August after falling 0.3 percent in July. The index for
    60  apparel was unchanged in August as was the index for recreation.
    61  The index for all items less food and energy rose 2.4 percent over the past 12 months. The shelter index increased 3.0
    62  percent over the last year. Other indexes with notable increases over the last year include airline fares (+23.4
    63  percent), recreation (+2.7 percent), medical care (+1.6 percent), and personal care (+3.8 percent).
    64  Not seasonally adjusted CPI measures
    65  The Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers (CPI-U) increased 3.4 percent over the last 12 months to an index level
    66  of 334.980 (1982-84=100). For the month, the index rose 0.3 percent prior to seasonal adjustment.
    67  The Consumer Price Index for Urban Wage Earners and Clerical Workers (CPI-W) increased 3.5 percent over the last 12
    68  months to an index level of 328.481 (1982-84=100). For the month, the index increased 0.4 percent prior to seasonal
    69  adjustment.
    70  The Chained Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers (C-CPI-U) increased 3.3 percent over the last 12 months. For
    71  the month, the index increased 0.3 percent on a not seasonally adjusted basis. Please note that the indexes for the past
    72  10 to 12 months are subject to revision.
    73  _______________
    74  The Consumer Price Index news release for September 2026 is scheduled to be published on Wednesday, October 14, 2026,
    75  at 8:30 a.m. (ET).
    76  Technical Note
    77  Brief Explanation of the CPI
    78  The Consumer Price Index (CPI) measures the change in prices paid by consumers for goods and services. The CPI reflects
    79  spending patterns for each of two population groups: all urban consumers and urban wage earners and clerical workers.
    80  The all urban consumer group represents over 90 percent of the total U.S. population. It is based on the expenditures
    81  of almost all residents of urban or metropolitan areas, including professionals, the self-employed, the poor, the
    82  unemployed, and retired people, as well as urban wage earners and clerical workers. Not included in the CPI are the
    83  spending patterns of people living in rural nonmetropolitan areas, farming families, people in the Armed Forces, and
    84  those in institutions, such as prisons and mental hospitals. Consumer inflation for all urban consumers is measured by
    85  two indexes, namely, the Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers (CPI-U) and the Chained Consumer Price Index for
    86  All Urban Consumers (C-CPI-U).
    87  The Consumer Price Index for Urban Wage Earners and Clerical Workers (CPI-W) is based on the expenditures of households
    88  included in the CPI-U definition that meet two requirements: more than one-half of the household's income must come
    89  from clerical or wage occupations, and at least one of the household's earners must have been employed for at least 37
    90  weeks during the previous 12 months. The CPI-W population represents approximately 30 percent of the total U.S.
    91  population and is a subset of the CPI-U population.
    92  The CPIs are based on prices of food, clothing, shelter, fuels, transportation, doctors' and dentists' services, drugs,
    93  and other goods and services that people buy for day-to-day living. Prices are collected each month in 75 urban areas
    94  across the country from about 6,000 housing units and approximately 22,000 retail establishments (department stores,
    95  supermarkets, hospitals, and other types of stores and service establishments). All taxes directly associated with the
    96  purchase and use of items are included in the index. Prices of fuels and a few other items are obtained every month in
    97  all 75 locations. Prices of most other commodities and services are collected every month in the three largest
    98  geographic areas and every other month in other areas. Prices of most goods and services are obtained by personal
    99  visit, telephone call, web, or app collection by the Bureau's trained representatives.
   100  In calculating the index, price changes for the various items in each location are aggregated using weights, which
   101  represent their importance in the spending of the appropriate population group. Local data are then combined to obtain
   102  a U.S. city average. For the CPI-U and CPI-W, separate indexes are also published by size of city, by region of the
   103  country, for cross-classifications of regions and population-size classes, and for 23 selected local areas. Area
   104  indexes do not measure differences in the level of prices among cities; they only measure the average change in prices
   105  for each area since the base period. For the C-CPI-U, data are issued only at the national level. The CPI-U and CPI-W
   106  are considered final when released, but the C-CPI-U is issued in preliminary form and subject to three subsequent
   107  quarterly revisions.
   108  The index measures price change from a designed reference date. For most of the CPI-U and the CPI-W, the reference base
   109  is 1982-84 equals 100. The reference base for the C-CPI-U is December 1999 equals 100. An increase of 7 percent from
   110  the reference base, for example, is shown as 107.000. Alternatively, that relationship can also be expressed as the
   111  price of a base period market basket of goods and services rising from $100 to $107.
   112  Sampling Error in the CPI
   113  The CPI is a statistical estimate that is subject to sampling error because it is based upon a sample of retail prices
   114  and not the complete universe of all prices. BLS calculates and publishes estimates of the 1-month, 2-month, 6-month,
   115  and 12-month percent change standard errors annually for the CPI-U. These standard error estimates can be used to
   116  construct confidence intervals for hypothesis testing. For example, the estimated standard error of the 1-month percent
   117  change is 0.04 percent for the U.S. all items CPI. This means that if we repeatedly sample from the universe of all
   118  retail prices using the same methodology, and estimate a percentage change for each sample, then 95 percent of these
   119  estimates will be within 0.08 percent of the 1-month percentage change based on all retail prices. For example, for a
   120  1-month change of 0.2 percent in the all items CPI-U, we are 95 percent confident that the actual percent change based
   121  on all retail prices would fall between 0.12 and 0.28 percent. For the latest data, including information on how to use
   122  the estimates of standard error, see www.bls.gov/cpi/tables/variance-estimates/home.htm.
   123  Calculating Index Changes
   124  Movements of the indexes from 1 month to another are usually expressed as percent changes rather than changes in index
   125  points, because index point changes are affected by the level of the index in relation to its base period, while
   126  percent changes are not. The following table shows an example of using index values to calculate percent changes:
   127  Item A Item B Item C
   128  Year I 112.500 225.000 110.000
   129  Year II 121.500 243.000 128.000
   130  Change in index points 9.000 18.000 18.000
   131  Percent change 9.0/112.500 x 100 = 8.0 18.0/225.000 x 100 = 8.0 18.0/110.000 x 100 = 16.4
   132  Use of Seasonally Adjusted and Unadjusted Data
   133  The Consumer Price Index (CPI) program produces both unadjusted and seasonally adjusted data. Seasonally adjusted data
   134  are computed using seasonal factors derived by the X-13ARIMA-SEATS seasonal adjustment method. These factors are
   135  updated each February, and the new factors are used to revise the previous 5 years of seasonally adjusted data. The
   136  factors are available at www.bls.gov/web/cpi/cpi-seasonal-factors.xlsx. For more information on data revision
   137  scheduling, please see the Seasonal Adjustment questions and answers page at
   138  www.bls.gov/cpi/seasonal-adjustment/questions-and-answers.htm and the Timeline of Seasonal Adjustment Methodological
   139  Changes at www.bls.gov/cpi/seasonal-adjustment/timeline-seasonal-adjustment-methodology-changes.htm.
   140  How to Use Seasonally Adjusted and Unadjusted Data
   141  For analyzing short-term price trends in the economy, seasonally adjusted changes are usually preferred since they
   142  eliminate the effect of changes that normally occur at the same time and in about the same magnitude every year-such as
   143  price movements resulting from weather events, production cycles, model changeovers, holidays, and sales. This allows
   144  data users to focus on changes that are not typical for the time of year.
   145  The unadjusted data are of primary interest to consumers concerned about the prices they actually pay. Unadjusted data
   146  are also used extensively for escalation purposes. Many collective bargaining contract agreements and pension plans,
   147  for example, tie compensation changes to the Consumer Price Index before adjustment for seasonal variation. BLS advises
   148  against the use of seasonally adjusted data in escalation agreements because seasonally adjusted series are revised
   149  annually for five years.
   150  Intervention Analysis
   151  The Bureau of Labor Statistics uses intervention analysis seasonal adjustment (IASA) for some CPI series. Sometimes
   152  extreme values or sharp movements can distort the underlying seasonal pattern of price change. Intervention analysis
   153  seasonal adjustment is a process by which the distortions caused by such unusual events are estimated and removed from
   154  the data prior to calculation of seasonal factors. The resulting seasonal factors, which more accurately represent the
   155  seasonal pattern, are then applied to the unadjusted data.
   156  For example, this procedure was used for the motor fuel series to offset the effects of the 2009 return to normal
   157  pricing after the worldwide economic downturn in 2008. Retaining this outlier data during seasonal factor calculation
   158  would distort the computation of the seasonal portion of the time series data for motor fuel, so it was estimated and
   159  removed from the data prior to seasonal adjustment. Following that, seasonal factors were calculated based on this
   160  "prior adjusted" data. These seasonal factors represent a clearer picture of the seasonal pattern in the data. The last
   161  step is for motor fuel seasonal factors to be applied to the unadjusted data.
   162  For the seasonal factors introduced for January 2026, BLS adjusted 57 series using intervention analysis seasonal
   163  adjustment, including selected food and beverage items, motor fuels and vehicles.
   164  Revision of Seasonally Adjusted Indexes
   165  Seasonally adjusted data, including the U.S. city average all items index levels, are subject to revision for up to 5
   166  years after their original release. Every year, economists in the CPI calculate new seasonal factors for seasonally
   167  adjusted series and apply them to the last 5 years of data. Seasonally adjusted indexes beyond the last 5 years of
   168  data are considered to be final and not subject to revision. For January 2026, revised seasonal factors and seasonally
   169  adjusted indexes for 2021 to 2025 were calculated and published. For series which are directly adjusted using the
   170  Census X-13ARIMA-SEATS seasonal adjustment software, the seasonal factors for 2025 will be applied to data for 2026 to
   171  produce the seasonally adjusted 2026 indexes. Series which are indirectly seasonally adjusted by summing seasonally
   172  adjusted component series have seasonal factors which are derived and are therefore not available in advance.
   173  Determining Seasonal Status
   174  Each year the seasonal status of every series is reevaluated based upon certain statistical criteria. Using these
   175  criteria, BLS economists determine whether a series should change its status from "not seasonally adjusted" to
   176  "seasonally adjusted", or vice versa. If any of the 81 components of the U.S. city average all items index change
   177  their seasonal adjustment status from seasonally adjusted to not seasonally adjusted, not seasonally adjusted data
   178  will be used in the aggregation of the dependent series for the last 5 years, but the seasonally adjusted indexes
   179  before that period will not be changed. For 2026, 36 of the 81 components of the U.S. city average all items index are
   180  not seasonally adjusted.
