     7  Statement on Longer-Run Goals and
     8  Monetary Policy Strategy
     9  Adopted effective January 24, 2012; as reaffirmed effective January 27, 2026
    10  
    11  The Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) is firmly committed to fulfilling its statutory mandate
    12  from Congress of promoting maximum employment, stable prices, and moderate long-term interest
    13  rates. The Committee seeks to explain its monetary policy decisions to the public as clearly as
    14  possible. Such clarity facilitates well-informed decisionmaking by households and businesses,
    15  reduces economic and financial uncertainty, increases the effectiveness of monetary policy, and
    16  enhances transparency and accountability, which are essential in a democratic society.
    17  
    18  The Committee’s monetary policy strategy is designed to promote maximum employment and stable
    19  prices across a broad range of economic conditions. Employment, inflation, and long-term interest
    20  rates fluctuate over time in response to economic and financial disturbances. Monetary policy plays
    21  an important role in stabilizing the economy in response to these disturbances. The Committee’s
    22  primary means of adjusting the stance of monetary policy is through changes in the target range for
    23  the federal funds rate. The Committee is prepared to use its full range of tools to achieve its
    24  maximum employment and price stability goals, particularly if the federal funds rate is constrained by
    25  its effective lower bound.
    26  
    27  Durably achieving maximum employment fosters broad-based economic opportunities and benefits for
    28  all Americans. The Committee views maximum employment as the highest level of employment that
    29  can be achieved on a sustained basis in a context of price stability. The maximum level of
    30  employment is not directly measurable and changes over time owing largely to nonmonetary factors
    31  that affect the structure and dynamics of the labor market. Consequently, it would not be appropriate
    32  to specify a fixed goal for employment; rather, the Committee’s policy decisions must be informed by
    33  assessments of the maximum level of employment, recognizing that such assessments are
    34  necessarily uncertain and subject to revision. The Committee considers a wide range of indicators in
    35  making these assessments.
    36  
    37  Price stability is essential for a sound and stable economy and supports the well-being of all
    38  Americans. The inflation rate over the longer run is primarily determined by monetary policy, and
    39  hence the Committee can specify a longer-run goal for inflation. The Committee reaffirms its judgment
    40  that inflation at the rate of 2 percent, as measured by the annual change in the price index for
    41  personal consumption expenditures, is most consistent over the longer run with the Federal Reserve’s
    42  statutory maximum employment and price stability mandates. The Committee judges that longer-term
    43  
    44  ---
    45  
    46  <!-- page 2 -->
    47  Statement on Longer Run Goals and Monetary Policy Strategy                                            2
    48  
    49  
    50  
    51  
    52  inflation expectations that are well anchored at 2 percent foster price stability and moderate long-term
    53  interest rates and enhance the Committee’s ability to promote maximum employment in the face of
    54  significant economic disturbances. The Committee is prepared to act forcefully to ensure that longer-
    55  term inflation expectations remain well anchored.
    56  
    57  Monetary policy actions tend to influence economic activity, employment, and prices with a lag.
    58  Moreover, sustainably achieving maximum employment and price stability depends on a stable
    59  financial system. Therefore, the Committee’s policy decisions reflect its longer-run goals, its medium-
    60  term outlook, and its assessments of the balance of risks, including risks to the financial system that
    61  could impede the attainment of the Committee’s goals.
    62  
    63  The Committee’s employment and inflation objectives are generally complementary. However, if the
    64  Committee judges that the objectives are not complementary, it follows a balanced approach in
    65  promoting them, taking into account the extent of departures from its goals and the potentially
    66  different time horizons over which employment and inflation are projected to return to levels judged
    67  consistent with its mandate. The Committee recognizes that employment may at times run above
    68  real-time assessments of maximum employment without necessarily creating risks to price stability.
    69  
    70  The Committee intends to review these principles and to make adjustments as appropriate at its
    71  annual organizational meeting each January, and to undertake roughly every 5 years a thorough public
    72  review of its monetary policy strategy, tools, and communication practices.

### data/sources/fed-rate/coverage/the-hill-decision-2026-09-16.txt
     1  Federal Reserve hikes interest rates to fight inflation
     2  
     3  Fed hikes interest rates by quarter point
     4  The Federal Reserve raised interest rates Wednesday for the first time in several years in the face of stubborn inflation.
     5  The central bank’s Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC), under Fed Chair Kevin Warsh, voted unanimously to increase the central bank’s benchmark interest rate to a range of 3.75 percent to 4 percent. It marks the first time the Fed has hiked rates in more than three years, and the first time in nearly a year since the range touched 4 percent.
     6  Warsh said after the decision that the rate hike will “support a timelier return” to the Fed’s 2 percent inflation target.
     7  “I would be hard pressed to describe broad financial conditions as restrictive,” he told reporters. “This view was widely shared by the committee, so we removed a dose of accommodation.”
     8  Looking ahead to the final two FOMC meetings of the year, a majority of committee officials project at least one rate hike. Twelve of 18 FOMC officials projected a single rate hike, according to a quarterly summary of economic projections the Fed released on Wednesday.
     9  Four officials projected two hikes, while two predicted a pair of holds. Warsh, who has strayed away from issuing forward guidance as Fed chair, did not provide a projection for the Fed’s last quarterly summary in June. He confirmed Wednesday that he again abstained from offering projections.
    10  Markets widely expected officials to raise rates by a quarter point on Wednesday in an effort to tame inflation, which remains above the Fed’s 2 percent target amid the war with Iran.
    11  But major stock indices still dipped after the decision, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average closing at a three-month low. The Nasdaq also fell by more than 130 points from Wednesday’s opening bell.
    12  Traders were pricing in a nearly 93 percent chance that the FOMC would raise rates by a quarter point Wednesday morning, according to the CME Group’s FedWatch tool, which tracks bets placed on future Fed decisions.
    13  Roughly 85 percent of economists Reuters surveyed over the weekend also predicted a quarter-point hike, a shift from the 70 percent of economists who predicted a hold before the Bureau of Labor Statistics reported annual inflation in August was 3.4 percent, as measured by the consumer price index (CPI).
    14  But the move could rankle Warsh’s relationship with President Trump. The president has long pushed for rate cuts, dating to the tenure of former Fed Chair Jerome Powell — who still sits on the FOMC and voted Wednesday to hike rates.
    15  Roughly four months before Powell’s eight-year tenure ended in May, the Justice Department launched a since-abandoned probe of the then-Fed chair over his handling of renovations to two of the central bank’s office buildings in Washington, D.C.
    16  Powell blasted the probe as an effort by the department, on Trump’s behalf, to pressure the Fed on monetary policy. The former Fed chair has also stayed on as a Fed board member amid Fed Inspector General Michael Horowitz’s investigation into the renovations.
    17  While Trump has given Warsh some room to maneuver in his first few meetings leading the central bank, he appears increasingly impatient for a cut.
    18  “Interest Rates in the United States should be 1%, or less, because we are the Best Credit in the World — BY FAR,” Trump posted on his Truth Social platform Wednesday, after the Fed rate announcement.
    19  Warsh had hinted at voting to raise interest rates last month, saying the central bank would have “work to do” if inflation was not moving toward its 2 percent target.
    20  “The responsibility for 65 months of sustained, elevated inflation sits squarely with the central bank. And that is where it belongs,” the Fed chair said at a summit in Jackson Hole, Wyo.
    21  While speaking to reporters on Wednesday, Warsh dodged two questions on Trump, chuckling in response to the first.
    22  But in speaking to the autonomy of the Fed from political influence, the chair said independence “is a two-way street.”
    23  Warsh added, “We let people that do trade policy and fiscal policy stay in their lane too. That’s how we can stand up here and call them the way we see them.”
    24  The Fed has slowly but surely lowered rates over the past few years, as the economy has recovered from a bout of severe inflation.
    25  Inflation surged after the COVID-19 pandemic, rising to a 40-year high of 9.1 percent in June 2022. The central bank began hiking rates in March 2022, although Powell faced criticism from experts for failing to move quickly enough to contain rising prices.
    26  The Fed repeatedly raised rates throughout 2022 and 2023, before settling at a multiyear high of 5.25 percent to 5.5 percent. It kept rates there for more than a year as inflation eased, before starting a series of cuts in late 2024.
    27  Roughly two years after reaching that four-decade high, inflation dipped to less than 3 percent and hovered between 2.3 percent and 3 percent for about 18 months.
    28  But it spiked after the U.S. and Israel launched the war with Iran, a conflict that has resulted in a shock to the oil industry due to the Islamic Republic’s restrictions on shipping in the Strait of Hormuz.
    29  Annual inflation hit a three-year high of 4.2 percent in May and has since fallen to 3.4 percent, according to the CPI.
    30  The Fed’s preferred measure of inflation, the personal consumption expenditures (PCE) price index, also hit 4.1 percent in May and was 3.7 percent in July, according to data from the Bureau of Economic Analysis. The bureau will release PCE data for August on Sept. 30.
    31  The Iran conflict, which hit the 200-day mark on Wednesday, also threatens to further increase inflation. The nonpartisan Congressional Budget Office reported Tuesday the war will increase its prior projection of annual inflation, as measured by the PCE, in the first quarter of 2027 by 0.5 percentage points.
    32  FOMC officials slightly tweaked their outlook for inflation, based on the committee’s quarterly summary of projections. The median projected inflation rate for this year was 3.7 percent, up from 3.6 percent in June.
    33  Officials also do not project inflation to dip to 2 percent until 2029, after projecting three months ago it will reach the Fed’s target by 2028.
    34  Loretta Mester, a former Cleveland Fed president, said there was a “cogent case” for a rate hike, adding incoming inflation data “are not convincing that underlying inflation is on a downward path” to 2 percent.
    35  Warsh was also resolute in saying the committee will deliver “price stability,” laying out the stakes if inflation remains above target.
    36  “The least well off are the ones who have the most to gain from stable prices,” he said, specifically naming those who do not own a home, have a 401(k) plan and are living paycheck-to-paycheck.
    37  “In stable prices, an environment where inflation is running consistent with our 2 percent objective offers good news because that way, when they get their wages, they can put their head above water and deliver real take-home pay increases,” Warsh added.
    38  Updated at 5 p.m. EDT
