data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-403-  >
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-404-<div class="bg-box">
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html:405:<p>Once every 10 years, following the release of decennial census data, states revise the legislative districts from which voters will elect members to the U.S. House of Representatives and their state legislatures for the next decade. Within the last year, however, states have undertaken mid-decade redistricting at rates not seen <a href="https://www.congress.gov/crs_external_products/IF/PDF/IF13082/IF13082.2.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">since the 1800s</a>. Since summer 2025, 10 states have changed their congressional maps: Alabama, California, Florida, Louisiana, Missouri, North Carolina, Ohio, Tennessee, Texas and Utah.</p>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-406-</div>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-407-<p>This page tracks states that make changes to district maps between the 2020 and 2030 redistricting cycles, beginning in late 2025. In many states, some officials have expressed interest in redistricting, but only some have undertaken a full redistricting process. States are added to this tracker when official redistricting action is taken, such as:</p>
--
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-743-</td>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-744-<td>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html:745:<p><a href="https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/2026RS/bills/hb/hb2100f.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">HB 2100</a> passed the legislature Aug. 4, 2026. The bill refers a constitutional amendment to the Nov. 3, 2026, ballot that would exempt congressional district maps from state redistricting criteria. The measure is widely understood to tee up congressional redistricting before the 2028 elections.</p>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-746-<p>The amendment would stipulate that all redistricting criteria in the state constitution apply only to state legislative district maps, not congressional maps. It would also give the legislature authority to grant original jurisdiction to the state supreme court to review congressional district maps.</p>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-747-<p>Maryland previously considered&mdash;and rejected&mdash; mid-decade redistricting. Introduced during the 2026 regular session, HB 488 would have created new maps and referred a state constitutional amendment to the Nov. 2026 ballot. Senate leadership opposed redistricting, and the session adjourned on April 13, 2026 without Senate action on redistricting legislation.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>
--
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-850-</td>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-851-<td>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html:852:<p>Before enacting the new 2026 maps, Tennessee lawmakers passed a law repealing a state law that prohibited <a href="/elections-and-campaigns/mid-decade-redistricting" target="_blank" rel="noopener">mid-decade congressional redistricting.</a></p>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-853-</td>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-854-</tr>
--
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-864-</td>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-865-<td>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html:866:<p>A November court order instructed Texas to use the maps enacted by the legislature in 2021 for the congressional midterm elections in 2026, finding that the 2026 maps were an unconstitutional racial gerrymander. On Nov. 21, the U.S. Supreme Court temporarily stayed that lower court order, while it considered the merits of the case. On Dec. 4, the Supreme Court issued a <a class="Hyperlink TrackedChange TrackChangeHyperlinkInstruction SCXW84138514 BCX0" href="https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/25a608_7khn.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">final stay</a> of the lower court&rsquo;s order, meaning the 2026 maps are in effect in Texas. Among other things, the court held that the lower court did not give the state legislature enough deference and that its ruling impermissibly interfered with Texas&rsquo;s ongoing congressional primary campaign.</p>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-867-</td>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-868-</tr>
--
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-906-</td>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-907-<td>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html:908:<p>The Washington Constitution delegates primary responsibility for drawing congressional maps to a redistricting commission. For the legislature to exercise authority over redistricting, lawmakers must amend the state constitution to allow the legislature to redistrict instead of the commission. The proposed amendment would authorize the legislature to implement new congressional maps anytime another state undertakes mid-decade redistricting absent a court order or other legal requirement. Washington&rsquo;s redistricting commission would continue to convene and draw new maps in each year ending in one. A constitutional amendment requires two-thirds approval in each chamber to be referred to the ballot. <a href="/about-state-legislatures/state-partisan-composition" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Neither chamber</a> has such a one-party supermajority.</p>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-909-</td>
data/sources/missouri-house-map/ncsl-mid-decade-redistricting.html-910-</tr>
--
